#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "332fefd8-7de7-5a84-81e2-a2aa97be9b73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Uniform Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "27a80de6-49d0-557b-8444-ebab046e795b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6074" :question/problem "What is the expected value of one roll of a tetrahedral die?" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nA tetrahedral die has four sides. Let $X$ be the score attained on a random roll of the die. Then the support is $S = \\{ 1,2,3,4 \\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1) = f(2) = f(3) = f(4) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90d0c882-c94b-5759-9a78-63979365e318" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Profit of a Game"}] :question/id #uuid "2b946455-893f-57db-b0a5-d52091914cf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6075" :question/problem "Suppose that we play a game with a stack of five cards labeled $1$ through $5.$ A card is drawn at random. Drawing $1,2,3,$ or $4$ means that you lose $\\[math]1,$ and drawing a $5$ means that you win $\\[/math] 5.$ What is the expected profit at each drawing in this game?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\[math]1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\[/math]5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5)  \\[5pt]\n&= -1 \\left( \\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\[3pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\[3pt]\n\t&=0.2\n\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\[math]0.20$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8297713-fb86-59f2-af4d-850af2575541" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Non-Uniform Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "e6dd60de-5df1-531d-9971-847343303fd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7383" :question/problem "The rolls of an unfair tetrahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{2}{5})+4(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{12}{10}+\\frac{8}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+6+12+8}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10} \\\\  & =2.7\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "ed80b546-956c-5b67-ac7d-0c0049c2278c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7385" :question/problem "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of pets | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $5$ | $6$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $5+6+5=16,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{16}=\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{5}{16})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{5}{16}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{6}{16}+\\frac{10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+6+10}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
